  "worked_solution": "Isolating the quadratic term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-6)^{2}+4 & \\ge 11 \\\\ (x-6)^{2} & \\ge 7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we take the square root of both sides and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{(x-6)^{2}} & \\ge \\sqrt{7} \\\\ |x-6| & \\ge \\sqrt{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the following compound inequality:\n\n$$\nx-6\\ge \\sqrt{7}\\,\\text{or}\\,x-6\\le -\\sqrt{7}\n$$\n\nWe can solve each inequality using the usual methods.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-6 & \\ge \\sqrt{7} &  &  & x-6 & \\le -\\sqrt{7} \\\\ x & \\ge \\sqrt{7}+6 &  &  & x & \\le -\\sqrt{7}+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\nx\\le -\\sqrt{7}+6\\,\\text{or}\\,x\\ge \\sqrt{7}+6.\n$$",
